title: 'pallet-xcm: weight asset claiming and precompile XCM input by their real size'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    Three related under-charges in XCM weighing, each exploitable to build blocks whose
    real execution cost far exceeds their accounted weight, plus a missing worst case in
    the `AliasOrigin` benchmark.

    **`claim_assets` extrinsic.** Charged a flat weight benchmarked with a single asset,
    while a claim may carry up to `MAX_ITEMS_IN_ASSETS` (20), each costing per-asset
    storage access through the `AssetTransactor`. It is now weighed linearly in the
    number of claimed assets.

    **`ClaimAsset` instruction.** Every runtime XCM weigher charged a flat single-asset
    weight; they now scale by the asset count, as `WithdrawAsset`, `BurnAsset` and
    `ExpectAsset` already did. This also closes the last gap in the inner message the
    `claim_assets` extrinsic builds, whose `DepositAsset` half already scaled.

    **XCM precompile input.** The `pallet-revive` XCM precompile charged a flat weight to
    decode and weigh a caller-supplied blob. `MAX_INSTRUCTIONS_TO_DECODE` does not bound
    that work: the weigher fully decodes any call carried by a `Transact` and recurses
    `get_dispatch_info()` over it, and every `DoubleEncoded` boundary starts a fresh
    instruction and decode-depth budget. `weighMessage`, `execute` and `send` now charge
    for the blob's size before decoding it. This charge is not refunded, so contracts
    forwarding large messages under a fixed gas limit may need a higher one.

    **`AliasOrigin` instruction.** Runtimes with `pallet_xcm::AuthorizedAliasers` last in
    their `Aliasers` benchmarked an origin/target pair matched by the first, storage-free
    filter, so the weight covered none of the authorized-alias lookup. They now set up the
    real worst case — a target holding the maximum number of aliasers, the matching one
    registered last and carrying an expiry — through the new
    `parachains_common::benchmarking` module, whose docs spell out the `Aliasers`
    configuration it assumes; chains configured differently have to work out their own.

    Breaking changes to `pallet_xcm::WeightInfo`, so chains with custom `pallet_xcm`
    weights must regenerate them:

    - `claim_assets` takes the asset count `n: u32`.
    - `weigh_message` takes the message length `n: u32`, and a new `decode_xcm(n)`
      measures decoding without weighing.

    Two defaulted `pallet_xcm::benchmarking::Config` hooks are also new. Chains whose
    `AssetTransactor` deposits several distinct asset kinds must override `get_assets`,
    or `claim_assets` measures a flat slope; the default is only sound for
    single-asset-kind transactors such as relay chains. Chains with a batching pallet
    must override `batch_call` (`utility.batch` in all in-tree runtimes), or the
    `weigh_message` worst case degrades and the precompile under-charges.

    `VersionedAssets` gained `len()` and `is_empty()`.
